// One quiz's questions, loaded on demand by group-quizzes/loader.js.
(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-renal-ii-comprehensive-quiz"]={"title":"Renal Physiology II — Comprehensive Quiz (75 Questions)","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"Which nephron segment is responsible for the majority of reabsorption of filtered water and sodium?","choices":["Thick ascending limb","Proximal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Early distal tubule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The PCT reabsorbs roughly 65% of the filtered water and sodium through isosmotic transport driven by the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase, making it the nephron's primary reabsorptive workhorse. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of filtered sodium is reabsorbed by the proximal convoluted tubule?","choices":["25%","10%","40%","65%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The PCT reabsorbs approximately 65% of filtered sodium via coupled transport with glucose, amino acids, and other solutes, driven by the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Which substance is almost completely reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule under normal physiological conditions?","choices":["Potassium","Urea","Creatinine","Glucose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Under normal conditions nearly 100% of filtered glucose is reabsorbed in the PCT via SGLT2 and SGLT1 transporters, up to the transport maximum; glucose appears in urine only when filtered load exceeds that maximum, as in hyperglycemia. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The descending limb of the Loop of Henle is characterized by:","choices":["High water permeability","Complete impermeability to water","High NaCl permeability","Active sodium transport"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The descending limb is richly lined with aquaporin-1 channels, making it highly permeable to water while remaining nearly impermeable to solutes, allowing osmotic equilibration with the surrounding medulla. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"As tubular fluid travels down the descending limb, it becomes:","choices":["Protein rich","More concentrated","More dilute","Isotonic to plasma throughout"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because the descending limb is water-permeable and the surrounding medullary interstitium becomes progressively more hyperosmotic with depth, water leaves the tubule and the fluid becomes progressively more concentrated. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Why does water leave the descending limb?","choices":["The surrounding medulla is hyperosmotic","Sodium is actively transported out","Active transport pumps water outward","ADH directly pumps water outward"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The hyperosmotic medullary interstitium, built by the countercurrent multiplier, osmotically draws water out of the water-permeable descending limb as the tubule descends deeper into the medulla. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The thick ascending limb is unique because it:","choices":["Actively transports Na⁺, K⁺, and Cl⁻","Is highly permeable to water","Performs glomerular filtration","Secretes renin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The TAL actively reabsorbs sodium, potassium, and chloride via the NKCC2 cotransporter while remaining impermeable to water, which both dilutes the tubular fluid and helps build the medullary osmotic gradient. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The transporter primarily responsible for sodium, potassium, and chloride reabsorption in the thick ascending limb is:","choices":["NCC","NKCC2","ENaC","ROMK"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. NKCC2, the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter, drives coupled reabsorption of sodium, potassium, and chloride across the apical membrane of the thick ascending limb, and is the target of loop diuretics like furosemide. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Why is the thick ascending limb called the diluting segment?","choices":["Water leaves faster than sodium","Sodium leaves while water cannot follow","It secretes water","Glucose is reabsorbed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The TAL actively pumps sodium (with potassium and chloride) out of the lumen, but because the segment is impermeable to water, water cannot follow, so the remaining tubular fluid becomes progressively hypo-osmotic (dilute). — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Which nephron segment contains the macula densa?","choices":["Collecting duct","Proximal tubule","Early distal convoluted tubule","Thin descending limb"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The macula densa is a specialized group of cells located in the early distal convoluted tubule, precisely where the tubule passes back by its own glomerulus and contacts the afferent and efferent arterioles. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The major sodium transporter found in the early distal convoluted tubule is:","choices":["ENaC","NCC","NKCC2","GLUT2"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The Na-Cl cotransporter (NCC) is the major sodium transporter of the early distal convoluted tubule and is the pharmacologic target of thiazide diuretics. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Parathyroid hormone primarily increases calcium reabsorption in the:","choices":["Collecting duct","Thick ascending limb","PCT","Early distal convoluted tubule"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. PTH stimulates calcium reabsorption in the early distal convoluted tubule by upregulating TRPV5 apical calcium channels, the principal hormonally regulated site of renal calcium handling. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The collecting duct becomes permeable to water primarily in response to:","choices":["ANP","Aldosterone","Renin","ADH"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ADH binds V2 receptors on principal cells, triggering a cAMP-mediated signaling cascade that inserts aquaporin-2 channels into the apical membrane, making the collecting duct permeable to water. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Without ADH, the collecting duct produces urine that is:","choices":["Dilute and high volume","Highly concentrated","Rich in protein","Small in volume"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Without ADH, aquaporin-2 channels are not inserted into the collecting duct, so water remains in the tubule and is excreted, producing large volumes of dilute urine — the hallmark of diabetes insipidus. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Which nephron segment makes the final adjustment in urine composition?","choices":["Descending limb","Glomerulus","Collecting duct","PCT"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The collecting duct performs the final, hormonally controlled adjustments to water, sodium, potassium, and acid-base balance (via ADH, aldosterone, and intercalated cells), fine-tuning urine composition before excretion. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The primary function of the macula densa is to monitor:","choices":["Plasma glucose","Potassium","NaCl delivery","Plasma proteins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Macula densa cells sense the rate of NaCl delivery to the distal tubule; this signal drives tubuloglomerular feedback and regulates renin release from adjacent juxtaglomerular cells. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells primarily release:","choices":["Aldosterone","ANP","Renin","ADH"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells of the afferent arteriole synthesize and release renin in response to reduced perfusion pressure, sympathetic stimulation, or low NaCl delivery sensed by the macula densa. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Low NaCl delivery to the macula densa results in:","choices":["Increased renin release","Increased ANP","Decreased sympathetic activity","Suppression of renin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low NaCl delivery to the macula densa signals reduced glomerular perfusion or filtration, prompting increased renin release from juxtaglomerular cells to activate RAAS and restore blood pressure and GFR. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Extraglomerular mesangial cells primarily function in:","choices":["Urine concentration","Glucose reabsorption","Potassium secretion","Communication between nephron structures"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Extraglomerular mesangial cells form a structural and signaling bridge between the macula densa and the juxtaglomerular cells/arterioles, relaying information within the juxtaglomerular apparatus. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The juxtaglomerular apparatus is primarily involved in regulating:","choices":["Erythropoiesis","Acid secretion","Blood pressure and GFR","Protein synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The JGA regulates blood pressure and GFR through renin release (activating RAAS) and tubuloglomerular feedback, which adjusts afferent arteriolar tone based on distal NaCl delivery. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"A patient becomes severely dehydrated after running a marathon. Which hormone will increase FIRST to conserve water?","choices":["ANP","Calcitonin","Insulin","ADH"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ADH responds within minutes to rising plasma osmolality sensed by hypothalamic osmoreceptors, making it the fastest hormonal responder to dehydration, well before the slower RAAS-driven aldosterone response takes full effect. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"A patient has syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH). Compared with normal, the urine would be expected to be:","choices":["Concentrated with low volume","Glucose rich","Protein rich","Dilute with high volume"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Excess ADH in SIADH drives maximal aquaporin-2-mediated water reabsorption in the collecting duct, producing concentrated, low-volume urine and dilutional hyponatremia in the blood. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"A mutation makes the thick ascending limb permeable to water. Which physiological process would be MOST impaired?","choices":["Renin release","Glomerular filtration","Calcium secretion","Countercurrent multiplication"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. If water could follow salt out of the TAL, the segment would lose its ability to dilute the tubular fluid without losing water, collapsing the medullary osmotic gradient and crippling the countercurrent multiplier system. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"A patient has damage to the proximal convoluted tubule. Which finding would be MOST likely?","choices":["Complete inability to secrete potassium","Increased renin secretion","Increased glucose loss in urine","Failure of ADH release"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The PCT is responsible for nearly complete glucose reabsorption via SGLT2/SGLT1; damage to this segment impairs that reabsorption, producing glycosuria, as seen classically in Fanconi syndrome. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"A patient has a lesion that destroys the thick ascending limb. Which combination of findings is MOST likely?","choices":["Loss of medullary osmotic gradient and difficulty concentrating urine","Increased ability to concentrate urine and increased NKCC2 activity","Increased glucose reabsorption and increased GFR","Increased ADH secretion with highly concentrated urine despite loss of the loop"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The TAL's active NaCl reabsorption is the engine that builds the medullary osmotic gradient; without it, the gradient collapses and the kidney loses its ability to concentrate urine even in the presence of ADH. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"ADH is synthesized primarily in the:","choices":["Anterior pituitary","Kidney","Posterior pituitary","Hypothalamus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ADH is synthesized by neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus, then transported down axons to the posterior pituitary for storage and release. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"ADH is released from the:","choices":["Posterior pituitary","Hypothalamus","Adrenal cortex","Juxtaglomerular cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. ADH synthesized in the hypothalamus is transported down axons and released into the circulation from axon terminals in the posterior pituitary. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Which stimulus is the MOST powerful for ADH secretion?","choices":["Decreased plasma osmolarity","Hypercalcemia","Increased GFR","Increased plasma osmolarity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rising plasma osmolarity is the single most potent stimulus for ADH release, sensed with great sensitivity by hypothalamic osmoreceptors. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"ADH acts primarily on:","choices":["Proximal tubule","Thick ascending limb","Late distal tubule and collecting duct","Thin descending limb"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. ADH acts on principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct, triggering aquaporin-2 insertion into the apical membrane to increase water reabsorption. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The primary effect of ADH is to:","choices":["Increase sodium secretion","Increase water reabsorption","Increase potassium secretion","Increase bicarbonate secretion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. ADH's principal action is to increase water reabsorption in the collecting duct by inserting aquaporin-2 channels, concentrating the urine and conserving body water. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"ADH inserts which channels into the collecting duct?","choices":["NKCC2","ENaC","Aquaporin-2","ROMK"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. ADH binds V2 receptors, raising intracellular cAMP and triggering the insertion of aquaporin-2 water channels into the apical membrane of collecting duct principal cells. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"A patient drinks six liters of water over two hours. Which response is MOST likely?","choices":["Increased aldosterone","Decreased ADH","Increased ADH","Increased renin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ingesting a large water load dilutes the plasma and lowers osmolality, which is sensed by hypothalamic osmoreceptors and suppresses ADH secretion, allowing the kidneys to excrete the excess water as dilute urine. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Without ADH, urine becomes:","choices":["Rich in glucose","Dilute with high volume","Small in volume","Highly concentrated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Without ADH, the collecting duct remains water-impermeable, so filtered water stays in the tubule and is excreted, producing large volumes of dilute urine. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Which disease results from inadequate ADH secretion?","choices":["SIADH","Diabetes mellitus","Diabetes insipidus","Conn syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Central diabetes insipidus results from inadequate hypothalamic/posterior pituitary ADH secretion, producing large volumes of dilute urine and significant thirst. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"A patient with SIADH would MOST likely produce urine that is:","choices":["Glucose-rich","Concentrated","Dilute","Protein-rich"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Excess ADH in SIADH maximizes water reabsorption in the collecting duct, producing concentrated urine alongside dilutional hyponatremia in the blood. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Aldosterone is produced by the:","choices":["Adrenal cortex (zona glomerulosa)","Kidney","Adrenal medulla","Posterior pituitary"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aldosterone is synthesized in the zona glomerulosa, the outermost layer of the adrenal cortex, in response to angiotensin II and elevated plasma potassium. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Aldosterone primarily acts on:","choices":["Principal cells","Thick ascending limb","Podocytes","Proximal tubule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aldosterone acts on principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct, upregulating ENaC and Na+/K+-ATPase to increase sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Aldosterone increases reabsorption of:","choices":["Calcium","Potassium","Hydrogen ions","Sodium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption in principal cells by upregulating ENaC and the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase, which in turn drives potassium and hydrogen ion secretion. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Aldosterone increases secretion of:","choices":["Calcium","Potassium","Sodium","Glucose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. By driving sodium reabsorption through ENaC and Na+/K+-ATPase in principal cells, aldosterone creates an electrochemical gradient that increases potassium (and hydrogen ion) secretion into the tubular lumen. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Water follows sodium because:","choices":["Osmosis","Active transport pulls water","Aquaporins pump water","ATP pumps water"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. As sodium is actively reabsorbed, it creates a local osmotic gradient that passively draws water along with it by osmosis, without any direct active transport of water itself. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Which condition would stimulate aldosterone release?","choices":["Hypernatremia","Low blood pressure","Hypervolemia","High ANP"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low blood pressure reduces renal perfusion, triggering renin release from JG cells, which activates RAAS and ultimately stimulates aldosterone secretion to restore sodium and volume. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"A patient with primary hyperaldosteronism would MOST likely have:","choices":["Hypercalcemia","Metabolic acidosis only","Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Excess aldosterone in primary hyperaldosteronism drives persistent potassium wasting through principal cell secretion, producing hypokalemia, along with hypertension and metabolic alkalosis from increased hydrogen ion secretion. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Renin is released from:","choices":["Macula densa","Mesangial cells","Juxtaglomerular cells","Podocytes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Renin is synthesized and released from juxtaglomerular (granular) cells of the afferent arteriole in response to low perfusion pressure, sympathetic stimulation, or low NaCl delivery detected by the macula densa. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Renin converts:","choices":["Angiotensinogen → Angiotensin I","ACE → Angiotensin II","Angiotensin I → Angiotensin II","Aldosterone → Renin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Renin cleaves angiotensinogen, a protein produced by the liver, into angiotensin I, the first step of the RAAS cascade. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"ACE is located primarily in the:","choices":["Liver","Kidney","Lung endothelium","Pancreas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin-converting enzyme is concentrated on the endothelium of pulmonary capillaries, where most of the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II occurs as blood passes through the lungs. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Angiotensin II causes:","choices":["Decreased aldosterone","Vasoconstriction","Vasodilation","Decreased thirst"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin II causes potent systemic vasoconstriction, raising blood pressure, and also stimulates aldosterone release, ADH release, and thirst as part of its integrated volume- and pressure-restoring actions. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Which arteriole does Angiotensin II preferentially constrict?","choices":["Efferent","Both equally","Afferent","Neither"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin II preferentially constricts the efferent arteriole, which raises glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure and helps maintain GFR even when renal perfusion is reduced. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Why does Angiotensin II preferentially constrict the efferent arteriole?","choices":["To increase Bowman's capsule pressure","To maintain glomerular hydrostatic pressure","To decrease renal blood flow completely","To lower GFR"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. By constricting the efferent arteriole preferentially, angiotensin II raises resistance downstream of the glomerulus, backing up pressure within the glomerular capillaries and maintaining glomerular hydrostatic pressure and filtration despite reduced overall renal blood flow. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone works AGAINST RAAS?","choices":["Cortisol","ADH","ANP","Epinephrine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial natriuretic peptide directly opposes RAAS, promoting natriuresis and diuresis, causing vasodilation, and suppressing both renin release and aldosterone secretion. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"A patient has severe hemorrhage. Which sequence is MOST likely?","choices":["↑ Renin → ↑ Ang II → ↑ Aldosterone","↓ Sympathetic Activity → ↓ Blood Pressure","↓ ADH → ↑ Urine Output","↑ ANP → ↓ Renin → ↓ Ang II"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hemorrhage lowers renal perfusion pressure, triggering increased renin release, which raises angiotensin II and subsequently aldosterone, all working to restore blood volume and pressure. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The primary purpose of the countercurrent multiplier system is to:","choices":["Increase GFR","Create a hyperosmotic renal medulla","Increase renal blood flow","Filter proteins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The countercurrent multiplier, driven by active NaCl transport in the thick ascending limb, builds and maintains the hyperosmotic medullary interstitium needed to concentrate urine under ADH's influence. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Which nephron segment actively establishes the medullary osmotic gradient?","choices":["Collecting duct","Thick ascending limb","Proximal convoluted tubule","Descending limb"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The thick ascending limb actively pumps NaCl out of the tubular lumen via NKCC2 while remaining impermeable to water, which is the driving force ('single effect') that establishes the medullary osmotic gradient. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The descending limb of the Loop of Henle is highly permeable to:","choices":["Water","Potassium","Glucose","Sodium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The descending limb is richly lined with aquaporin-1 channels, giving it high water permeability that allows osmotic equilibration with the surrounding hyperosmotic medulla. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The ascending limb is characterized by:","choices":["Glucose secretion","Active salt reabsorption","Protein filtration","Water permeability"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The ascending limb (particularly the thick portion) actively reabsorbs NaCl via NKCC2 while remaining impermeable to water, diluting the tubular fluid and helping build the medullary gradient. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The \"single effect\" of the countercurrent multiplier refers to:","choices":["Glomerular filtration","ADH secretion","Water movement through aquaporins","Active pumping of NaCl from the ascending limb"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The 'single effect' specifically refers to the active transport of NaCl out of the ascending limb into the medullary interstitium; this effect is repeated along the length of the loop, multiplying the gradient via countercurrent flow. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Fluid displacement refers to:","choices":["Potassium secretion","Protein filtration","Sodium filtration","Movement of filtrate through the Loop of Henle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fluid displacement describes the ongoing flow of tubular fluid through the loop of Henle, which, combined with the repeated 'single effect' of active salt transport, multiplies the small local osmotic gradient into the large corticomedullary gradient. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The corticomedullary osmotic gradient is essential because it allows:","choices":["Renin release","Glucose filtration","ADH to reabsorb water effectively","Protein secretion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The corticomedullary osmotic gradient provides the osmotic driving force that lets ADH-inserted aquaporin-2 channels pull water out of the collecting duct, concentrating the urine. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Without the medullary osmotic gradient:","choices":["Albumin enters urine","Potassium secretion stops","ADH cannot effectively concentrate urine","GFR increases dramatically"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Without the osmotic gradient built by the countercurrent multiplier, there is no osmotic force to pull water out of the collecting duct, so even with ADH present and aquaporin-2 inserted, the kidney cannot concentrate the urine. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Which structure preserves the medullary osmotic gradient?","choices":["Juxtaglomerular cells","Bowman's capsule","Vasa recta","Afferent arteriole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The vasa recta, running parallel to the loop of Henle, act as a countercurrent exchanger — their hairpin blood flow removes reabsorbed water and solute from the medulla without washing out (dissipating) the osmotic gradient. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The vasa recta functions primarily as:","choices":["The countercurrent multiplier","The countercurrent exchanger","The source of renin","The filtration barrier"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The vasa recta function as a countercurrent exchanger, using their parallel hairpin blood flow to passively remove excess water and solute from the medulla while preserving the osmotic gradient established by the loop of Henle. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"The four major renal processes include all EXCEPT:","choices":["Filtration","Secretion","Reabsorption","Digestion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The four major renal processes are filtration, reabsorption, secretion, and excretion; digestion is a gastrointestinal process and is not one of the kidney's core functions. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Which equation correctly describes urinary excretion?","choices":["Reabsorption − Filtration","Secretion − Filtration","Filtration + Reabsorption − Secretion","Filtration − Reabsorption + Secretion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Excretion equals the amount filtered, minus what is reabsorbed back into the blood, plus what is additionally secreted into the tubule. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"If glucose is filtered and completely reabsorbed:","choices":["Glucose excretion equals zero","Glucose appears in urine","Glucose excretion equals filtration","Glucose secretion increases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Using excretion = filtration − reabsorption + secretion, if reabsorption equals the full filtered load and there is no secretion of glucose, then excretion equals zero — no glucose appears in the urine. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"If secretion of a substance increases while filtration remains unchanged:","choices":["Filtration decreases","Reabsorption increases automatically","Excretion increases","Excretion decreases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Since excretion = filtration − reabsorption + secretion, increasing secretion while filtration (and, implicitly, reabsorption) stays the same directly raises the total amount excreted. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Principal cells are located primarily in the:","choices":["Bowman's capsule","Proximal tubule","Late distal tubule and collecting duct","Thick ascending limb"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Principal cells reside in the late distal tubule and collecting duct, where they mediate aldosterone-regulated sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion, and ADH-regulated water reabsorption. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Principal cells primarily regulate:","choices":["Calcium","Glucose","Sodium and potassium","Proteins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Principal cells mediate aldosterone-driven sodium reabsorption via ENaC and potassium secretion via ROMK, making them central to sodium and potassium balance. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Aldosterone increases activity of:","choices":["Carbonic anhydrase","GLUT2 transporters","NKCC2 transporters","ENaC channels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aldosterone increases the activity and expression of ENaC (the epithelial sodium channel) and the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase in principal cells, driving sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Activation of principal cells results in:","choices":["Increased Na⁺ reabsorption and K⁺ secretion","Increased glucose secretion","Increased bicarbonate secretion","Increased K⁺ reabsorption"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Activated principal cells reabsorb sodium via ENaC while secreting potassium via ROMK, the two hallmark, coupled actions of this cell type under aldosterone stimulation. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Intercalated cells primarily regulate:","choices":["Acid-base balance","Water","Glucose","Sodium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Intercalated cells (types A and B) are specialized for acid-base regulation, secreting hydrogen ions or bicarbonate as needed to maintain systemic pH. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Type A intercalated cells are activated primarily during:","choices":["Hypokalemia","Acidosis","Hypernatremia","Alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Type A intercalated cells are activated during acidosis, secreting hydrogen ions into the tubular lumen and reabsorbing newly generated bicarbonate to help correct the low pH. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Type A intercalated cells:","choices":["Secrete glucose","Secrete bicarbonate","Secrete sodium","Secrete hydrogen ions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Type A intercalated cells secrete hydrogen ions into the tubular lumen via an apical H+-ATPase, helping to correct acidosis and generate new bicarbonate for reabsorption. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Type B intercalated cells primarily:","choices":["Reabsorb potassium","Secrete hydrogen ions","Reabsorb glucose","Secrete bicarbonate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Type B intercalated cells secrete bicarbonate into the tubular lumen (while reabsorbing hydrogen ions), activated during alkalosis to help correct elevated systemic pH. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of filtered bicarbonate is reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule?","choices":["40%","60%","80–90%","20%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The PCT reclaims approximately 80–90% of filtered bicarbonate via a carbonic anhydrase-dependent mechanism, with the remainder reabsorbed and fine-tuned by more distal segments, including the intercalated cells. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"Which enzyme is essential for bicarbonate reabsorption?","choices":["ACE","Carbonic anhydrase","Renin","Aldosterone synthase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carbonic anhydrase catalyzes the interconversion of CO2, water, carbonic acid, and bicarbonate/hydrogen ions, a reaction essential to both proximal tubular bicarbonate reabsorption and intercalated cell acid-base handling. — Renal Physiology II lecture"},{"q":"A patient develops severe metabolic acidosis after prolonged diarrhea. Which combination of renal responses would best compensate?","choices":["↑ sodium excretion only","↓ H⁺ secretion, ↓ bicarbonate reabsorption","↑ H⁺ secretion, ↑ bicarbonate reabsorption, ↑ new bicarbonate generation","↓ potassium secretion only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. To compensate for metabolic acidosis from diarrheal bicarbonate losses, the kidney increases type A intercalated cell hydrogen ion secretion, maximizes bicarbonate reabsorption, and generates new bicarbonate to restore acid-base balance. — Renal Physiology II lecture"}]};
